// docs/spec/en/07-constexpr-and-consteval.md (ch09) §9.1(4): every
// potentially-evaluated call to an immediate function shall produce a
// constant expression. A default member initializer is such an expression;
// it is evaluated for every constructor that does not name the member in its
// mem-initializer-list ([class.base.init]), so the immediate call folds
// there.
// docs/spec/en/06-constant-evaluation.md (ch06) §7.2(6.2) requires the
// implementation to support the call.
//
// Call position: default member initializer. Callee: free `consteval`
// function.
//
// Expected value derived from the spec text above and independently confirmed
// with `clang++-22 -std=c++26`, which compiles this exact source and exits 42.
//
// CURRENT STATUS (scpp @ a59ffce): FAILS. scpp reports at `int
// value{twice(21)};` -- "error: internal error: no generated code for
// resolved function 'twice' called here", i.e. the default member initializer
// is lowered as a runtime call instead of being folded. The expectation above
// comes from the spec and from clang; do not weaken it to match the
// implementation.
consteval int twice(int x) {
    return x * 2;
}

class Box {
public:
    virtual ~Box() = default;
private:
    int value{twice(21)};
public:
    Box() {
        return;
    }
    int get() const {
        return this->value;
    }
};

int main() {
    Box b{};
    return b.get();
}
